= Noosphere =

The '''noosphere''' is a historical philosophical and scientific concept describing a planetary domain or stage associated with human thought, reason, knowledge, and the increasing influence of human activity on the biosphere. The term emerged during intellectual exchanges involving Vladimir Vernadsky, Édouard Le Roy, and Pierre Teilhard de Chardin in the 1920s, but those authors developed significantly different theories.

Vernadsky integrated the noosphere with biogeochemistry and the geological influence of scientific humanity. Teilhard placed it inside a teleological and partly theological vision of cosmic evolution toward increasing complexity, consciousness, and an Omega Point. These formulations should not be collapsed into one scientific theory.<ref name="review">[https://doi.org/10.1002/sres.2997 Vidal, C. et al. (2023), “What is the noosphere? Planetary superorganism, major evolutionary transition and emergence,” ''Systems Research and Behavioral Science''.]</ref>

Human activity is demonstrably a planetary geological and ecological force. That observation does not establish a literal sphere of thought, a unified planetary mind, an inevitable evolutionary transition, or a predetermined spiritual endpoint.

== Origin of the term ==

The concept developed through interaction among Vernadsky, Le Roy, and Teilhard in Paris during the 1920s.

Vernadsky lectured on geochemistry and the biosphere at the Sorbonne in 1922–1923. Le Roy and Teilhard, influenced by Henri Bergson as well as evolutionary thought, discussed the emergence of a new sphere associated with human thought.

Historical research indicates that Teilhard used the word in an unpublished 1925 text and Le Roy was the first to publish it in lectures later issued in print.<ref name="review" />

Vernadsky subsequently adopted and transformed the concept within his own biogeochemical framework.

The history is collaborative enough that assigning the idea to one sole inventor is misleading.

== Vernadsky's noosphere ==

Vernadsky viewed life as a geological force that transforms Earth's surface through biogeochemical processes.

Humanity introduces a new form of planetary transformation because scientific thought, technology, agriculture, industry, and social organization can reorganize matter and energy at unprecedented scales.

In ''Scientific Thought as a Planetary Phenomenon'', written primarily in the late 1930s, Vernadsky described scientific knowledge as contributing to a new geological state of the biosphere.<ref name="vernadsky">[https://revistas.usp.br/khronos/en/article/view/128883 Vernadsky, V. I., ''Scientific Thought as a Planetary Phenomenon'', historical text; 2017 published translation excerpt and publication history.]</ref>

This framing can be interpreted without assuming a conscious planet.

Its strongest empirical component is the claim that organized human activity has become geologically significant.

== Teilhard de Chardin's noosphere ==

Teilhard's noosphere is part of a larger evolutionary philosophy.

He described matter, life, and thought as stages in a process of increasing complexity and interiority. Human minds become increasingly interconnected, producing a noosphere that develops toward greater integration.

His system culminates in the '''Omega Point''', a proposed final convergence of consciousness with explicitly theological dimensions.

Modern scholarship emphasizes that Teilhard's work combines evolutionary language with Christian theology; the Omega hypothesis cannot be treated as an empirical conclusion derived from evolutionary biology.<ref name="grumett">[https://doi.org/10.1177/0040571X19872102 Grumett, D. (2019), “Pierre Teilhard de Chardin's The Phenomenon of Man,” ''Theology'' 122(6), 404–411.]</ref>

Teilhard's framework remains philosophically and historically influential, but its teleological claims are not established scientific laws.

== Vernadsky and Teilhard compared ==

The two traditions overlap in several themes:

* humanity as a planetary phenomenon;
* increasing importance of scientific knowledge;
* transformation of the biosphere;
* historical evolution toward new organizational conditions.

But they differ substantially.

'''Vernadsky''' emphasizes biogeochemistry, scientific activity, and geological transformation.

'''Teilhard''' emphasizes consciousness, spiritual evolution, convergence, and teleology.

The distinction matters because empirical evidence for anthropogenic geological effects does not validate Teilhard's theological cosmology.

== Human activity as geological force ==

Human impacts on Earth can be studied independently of noosphere theory.

Measurable processes include:

* fossil-fuel combustion;
* land transformation;
* nitrogen fixation;
* mining;
* dam construction;
* biodiversity change;
* material production;
* radionuclide dispersal;
* atmospheric composition change.

These observations motivate modern concepts such as the Anthropocene and technosphere.

The noosphere is not required to describe those physical changes, but Vernadsky's emphasis on humanity as a planetary geological force was historically significant.

== Scientific thought as planetary process ==

Vernadsky treated science as a collective historical process capable of altering human action at planetary scale.

Scientific knowledge is not merely stored inside individual minds. It depends on:

* institutions;
* communication;
* instruments;
* education;
* archives;
* international collaboration.

This aspect connects the noosphere to modern [[Knowledge ecosystem|knowledge ecosystems]] and scholarly communication.

Yet a planet-spanning scientific network remains socially fragmented and politically contested.

== Communication and integration ==

Later interpreters associate the Internet and global communication networks with noospheric development.

Digital systems clearly increase the speed and scale at which ideas, data, and coordination can travel.

However,

<pre>
global communication
≠ unified knowledge
≠ unified cognition
≠ unified consciousness
</pre>

Connectivity can also amplify misinformation, surveillance, polarization, and conflict.

== Noosphere and global brain ==

The [[Global brain]] is a later technological and cybernetic metaphor emphasizing distributed information processing through human–computer networks.

The noosphere has broader philosophical and historical roots and does not inherently require digital technology.

The two concepts overlap in predictions of increasing planetary interconnection but should remain separate knowledge objects.

== Noosphere and infosphere ==

The [[Infosphere]] generally refers to informational environments and informational entities.

It does not necessarily imply an evolutionary stage of the biosphere or increasing collective reason.

The noosphere is specifically tied to human thought, planetary history, and theories of evolutionary transformation.

== Noosphere and collective intelligence ==

[[Collective intelligence]] is experimentally studied as group-level task performance.

Evidence that groups can aggregate judgments or collaborate effectively supports bounded claims about collective performance.

It does not establish a noosphere.

A noosphere interpretation adds planetary scale and historical-evolutionary claims requiring separate evidence.

== Noosphere and ecology ==

Vernadsky's concept connects human knowledge with physical transformation of the [[Ecology|biosphere]].

This can encourage analysis of feedback between:

* scientific knowledge;
* technology;
* economic organization;
* environmental impact;
* governance.

Such analysis is valuable without assuming a literal cognitive shell around Earth.

== Teleology ==

A major epistemic issue is teleology.

A teleological theory claims that evolution is directed toward an end.

Modern evolutionary biology does not establish that biological or cultural evolution must culminate in collective consciousness, a noosphere, or an Omega Point.

Increasing technological connectivity is historically contingent and can reverse.

Predictions of inevitable convergence should therefore be classified as philosophical or speculative.

== Planetary superorganism interpretations ==

Some contemporary systems theorists interpret the noosphere through superorganism or major-transition theory.<ref name="review" />

These approaches attempt to ask whether humanity is becoming integrated enough to form a higher-level evolutionary individual.

A genuine major evolutionary transition would require explicit criteria, potentially including:

* suppression of lower-level conflict;
* heritable organization;
* division of labor;
* collective reproduction;
* shared fitness interests.

Human civilization does not straightforwardly satisfy organismal criteria.

The analogy remains an active theoretical proposal.

== Ethical and political dimensions ==

A noosphere framed as increasing integration can hide questions of power.

Who controls communication infrastructure?

Whose knowledge is preserved?

Which languages dominate?

Who determines planetary goals?

Technological integration can coexist with inequality and coercion.

Noosphere narratives should therefore avoid treating unification as automatically desirable.

== Research use ==

The concept can be useful in:

* history of science;
* environmental history;
* philosophy of technology;
* Earth-systems thought;
* cultural evolution;
* studies of global knowledge networks.

A rigorous study should identify which version of the noosphere is being used.

A claim about material planetary transformation requires Earth-system evidence.

A claim about knowledge diffusion requires social and communication data.

A claim about collective consciousness requires an independent theory and evidence of consciousness.

== Epistemic status ==

The noosphere is classified here as '''Hypothetical''' as a planetary evolutionary entity.

Its intellectual history is documented, and measurable human transformation of Earth is established.

The stronger propositions that humanity is becoming one higher-order organism, that planetary consciousness exists, or that evolution is destined toward noospheric convergence remain unverified.

